const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Experimental Design Service","serviceType":"Experimental study design, protocol development and methodology consulting","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/experimental-design/","description":"The right design chosen from your constraints, with the sample size justified in writing before the first participant. From $420, in 5–7 days.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose the right design for my research objective?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By working from the constraints rather than the preferences, in roughly this order. Is the intervention delivered to individuals or to whole groups — that alone decides whether you are randomising patients or clinics. Can it be withheld from anyone, or is it coming to everybody regardless, which points at a stepped wedge or an interrupted time series. Can one participant receive both conditions in sequence, which is the question a crossover lives or dies on. How many can you realistically recruit. And what will your ethics committee accept. Only when those are answered does the choice between a parallel trial, a crossover, a factorial or a quasi-experimental design become obvious — and it usually is obvious at that point, which is why the first conversation is short and the consequences of skipping it are not."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the key principles of experimental design?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Four, and they were settled long before modern statistics. Control: something to compare against, so that the effect of the intervention can be separated from what would have happened anyway. Randomisation: allocation by chance, which balances the confounders you know about and, uniquely among methods, the ones you do not. Replication: enough units to distinguish a real effect from noise, which is what a sample size calculation is for. Blocking or stratification: removing a known source of variation from the comparison by design rather than adjusting for it afterwards. Blinding and allocation concealment are then the operational protections that stop these principles being undone in practice — and concealment, which is about the moment of allocation rather than what happens after it, is the one most often confused with blinding and most often missing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a true experimental and a quasi-experimental design?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Randomisation. A true experimental design allocates participants or clusters to conditions by chance, which is what allows the groups to be assumed comparable on everything — measured and unmeasured. A quasi-experimental design compares groups that were formed some other way: by policy, by geography, by who volunteered, by what happened before and after a date. That does not make it weak, and the good quasi-experimental designs are considerably stronger than most people assume: interrupted time series, difference-in-differences and regression discontinuity all have ways of ruling out specific alternative explanations. What they cannot do is rule out an unmeasured confounder by design, so the argument has to be made explicitly and defended. Choose one when randomisation is impractical, premature or ethically impossible — and say which of those three it was."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When should I use a crossover design?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When the condition is chronic and stable, the treatment effect is reversible, and the outcome can be measured more than once. Those three conditions together are less common than researchers hope, and each one that fails is fatal rather than inconvenient. A cure, a surgical procedure, a vaccine or anything that changes behaviour permanently cannot be crossed over. Where they do hold, the gain is the largest in trial design: each participant serves as their own control, between-person variation leaves the comparison entirely, and the trial commonly needs a quarter to a half the participants. The cost is carry-over — if the first treatment is still acting in the second period, the estimate is biased — so the washout has to be justified from how the treatment actually clears, not from what fits the schedule."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is sample size determined, and how do I avoid getting it wrong?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"From four things: the outcome's baseline value, the difference worth detecting, the power you want and the significance level you will test at. The arithmetic is the easy part and the assumptions are where studies go wrong. The three most common errors are using an effect size chosen because it makes the study affordable rather than because it is clinically meaningful; taking a control rate from a different population without saying so; and forgetting to inflate. Inflation matters twice over — for attrition, and for clustering. A cluster-randomised trial needs its sample multiplied by the design effect, which with thirty participants per cluster and a modest ICC of 0.02 is a fifty-eight per cent increase. That single omission has under-powered more cluster trials than any other mistake, and it is invisible until the analysis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What randomisation method should I use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Rarely simple randomisation, which is what most people mean by the word. Tossing a coin for each participant leaves real imbalance at realistic sample sizes — with sixty participants, a split as uneven as 38:22 turns up more often than researchers expect. Blocked randomisation keeps the arms level throughout recruitment; stratified blocking does the same within important prognostic groups; minimisation allocates each participant to whichever arm keeps the overall balance best and is the strongest option for small trials with several important factors. Whichever you choose, the part that actually protects the study is concealment: the sequence generated by someone not recruiting, and the next allocation unknowable until the participant is irreversibly enrolled. A perfect sequence that a recruiting clinician can see is not randomisation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the most common pitfalls in planning an experiment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Seven, in roughly this order of frequency. A sample size chosen from what is affordable and justified afterwards. No control group where one was possible. Outcomes defined loosely enough that several measures could be reported and the best one chosen. Clustering ignored in the design and again in the analysis. Allocation not concealed, so recruiters could anticipate the next arm. Attrition unplanned for, with no strategy for the people who leave. And a primary outcome that was changed between protocol and paper — which registries now make visible and reviewers now check. Six of the seven are decided before the first participant, and the seventh is decided by whether the first six were written down."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to register my study, and when?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If it is a clinical trial, yes, and before the first participant is enrolled. Every ICMJE member journal requires prospective registration as a condition of considering the paper, and registering afterwards is permanently visible in the record — the registry shows the date. Use ClinicalTrials.gov, CTRI in India, ISRCTN, the EU CTIS or whichever registry your region and your funder require. Beyond trials, registration is becoming expected more broadly: PROSPERO for systematic reviews, and the Open Science Framework for observational and preclinical protocols, where pre-registration is increasingly what separates a confirmatory finding from an exploratory one in a reviewer's mind. Registration costs nothing and takes an afternoon once the protocol exists, which is the argument for writing the protocol properly first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help if the study has already started?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and how much depends on where you are. Before recruitment opens, almost everything is still changeable and a protocol amendment is routine. During recruitment, the design is fixed but the analysis plan, the handling of missing data and the reporting can still be settled properly — and writing them down mid-recruitment is far better than writing them after the data is visible. After the data is collected, we can advise on what the study can honestly support, which is sometimes less than was hoped, and on how to report that without overstating it. The one thing that cannot be done is change what a completed design is capable of answering. If you suspect the design is wrong, the time to ask is now rather than at write-up, when the options narrow to reporting it accurately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does expert support improve interpretation and reporting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Mostly by making the reporting match the design, which sounds trivial and is where most manuscripts lose credibility. The specific failures are consistent: causal language used for an observational design; a secondary outcome reported as though it were the primary; subgroup findings presented without the interaction test; clustering analysed as though participants were independent; and a limitations paragraph that lists generic caveats rather than the actual weaknesses of this design. Someone who built the design knows exactly where it was compromised and can write those limitations honestly — which reviewers reward, because a paper that names its own weaknesses is far more convincing than one a reviewer has to find them in. The whole thing is then written to CONSORT, STROBE or ARRIVE, with the checklist completed item by item rather than ticked."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a pilot study for, and can I use it to estimate my effect size?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A pilot tests feasibility: whether you can recruit, whether the measurement works, whether the protocol survives contact with a real site. It is not powered to estimate an effect, and using its effect size to power the main study is one of the most common and most costly errors in research planning — small samples produce wildly variable estimates, and the ones that look promising enough to justify a trial are disproportionately the ones that overshot. Use the pilot for the variance and the recruitment rate, and take the effect worth detecting from what would change practice or from a published, adequately powered source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I handle clustering — clinics, cages, classrooms?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By recognising it in the design rather than patching it in the analysis. If the intervention is delivered to a group, the group is the experimental unit, and analysing individuals as though they were independent inflates the apparent sample size and produces confidence intervals that are far too narrow. The design needs an intracluster correlation coefficient and a design effect, which in a cluster-randomised trial routinely doubles or triples the number of participants required. Getting this wrong is not a subtle statistical point; it is the difference between a study that can answer the question and one that cannot, and it is visible to any reviewer who counts the clusters."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a factorial design, and when is the complexity worth it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A factorial design tests two or more interventions in the same participants, so that with four arms you answer two questions for roughly the cost of one. It is efficient when the interventions are expected to act independently, and each main effect is estimated on the full sample. It stops being efficient the moment you need to detect the interaction between them, because powering for an interaction typically needs around four times the sample of a main effect. So the practical rule is: use it when you genuinely expect no interaction and would be content to report the main effects, and be explicit in the protocol that the study is not powered to detect one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between superiority, non-inferiority and equivalence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What you are trying to show, which changes the analysis, the sample size and the conclusion you are allowed to draw. A superiority trial asks whether the new treatment is better; failing to show it is not evidence that the treatments are the same. A non-inferiority trial asks whether the new one is not worse by more than a pre-specified margin, and that margin is a clinical judgement made and justified before the trial, not a statistical convenience chosen afterwards. Equivalence bounds the difference in both directions. The commonest error is running a superiority trial, finding p > 0.05 and concluding equivalence, which the design does not support."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do observational studies need to be registered?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by regulation in most jurisdictions, and increasingly by expectation. A growing number of journals ask whether an observational study was registered and, if it was not, treat the analysis as exploratory — which is a materially weaker claim. Registration on ClinicalTrials.gov, OSF or a comparable platform costs little and does one valuable thing: it establishes that the primary outcome and the analysis plan existed before the data were examined. That is the single defence against the accusation reviewers cannot otherwise rule out, which is that the reported comparison was the one that worked. We register at protocol stage where you want it, and we say in the paper when registration came after data collection."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Experimental design","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/experimental-design/"}]}];

